// Active Directory Threat Detection Lab - detection searches
// Author: John Smith Jr
// Logs forwarded from DC01 and WIN11-01 (homelab.local) to Splunk (SPLUNK-01).
// Adjust index names to match your environment.

// =====================================================================
// 1. PASSWORD SPRAY - failed logons across many accounts from one source
// =====================================================================
// Event 4625 = failed logon. A spray shows as many distinct target
// accounts failing from a single source in a short window.
// Tune the > 3 threshold and the time window to your lab.

index=wineventlog sourcetype="WinEventLog:Security" EventCode=4625
  | bin _time span=10m
  | stats count dc(Account_Name) as targeted_accounts values(Account_Name) as accounts by Source_Network_Address, _time
  | where targeted_accounts > 3
  | sort - targeted_accounts

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  // 1a. Account lockout confirmation (the configured control firing)
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  // Event 4740 = account locked out. Correlating with the 4625 burst
  // above shows the lockout policy actually tripped.

  index=wineventlog sourcetype="WinEventLog:Security" EventCode=4740
  | stats count by Account_Name, _time
    | sort - _time

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    // 1b. Spray followed by a success (investigate these first)
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    // A 4624 success from the same source that generated many 4625s means
    // a guessed credential landed.

    index=wineventlog sourcetype="WinEventLog:Security" (EventCode=4625 OR EventCode=4624)
    | stats count(eval(EventCode=4625)) as failures count(eval(EventCode=4624)) as successes by Source_Network_Address, Account_Name
    | where failures > 5 AND successes > 0

    // =====================================================================
    // 2. POWERSHELL SCRIPT-BLOCK LOGGING - what a script actually did
    // =====================================================================
    // Event 4104 = script block logged. Script-block logging was enabled
    // via GPO; this surfaces the content, not just that PowerShell ran.

    index=wineventlog sourcetype="WinEventLog:Microsoft-Windows-PowerShell/Operational" EventCode=4104
    | search ScriptBlockText IN ("*DownloadString*","*IEX*","*Invoke-Expression*","*-enc*","*FromBase64String*")
    | table _time, Computer, ScriptBlockText

      // =====================================================================
      // 3. LLMNR / RESPONDER - documented GAP, not a working detection
      // =====================================================================
      // Responder poisoning happens at the network layer (LLMNR / NBT-NS)
      // and leaves little in the Windows Security log. In this lab the attack
      // succeeded while these host-based sources stayed quiet - the finding
      // was the visibility gap itself.
      //
      // Remediation is to remove the attack surface via GPO, not detect after:
      //   - Turn off Multicast Name Resolution (disables LLMNR)
      //   - Disable NetBIOS over TCP/IP on adapters
      // Re-run Responder afterward to confirm no hash is captured.
      
